{"@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[
{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need an architect to build a house in Ontario?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Generally no. Houses are exempt from the requirement for an architect, so a BCIN designer qualified in the House category can prepare the drawings and apply for the permit. You can still choose to work with an architect."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"What does BCIN mean?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Building Code Identification Number. It shows that a designer has passed the Province's Building Code exams and is registered with the Ministry of Municipal Affairs and Housing in specific categories."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"Can a BCIN designer do permit drawings for a basement apartment?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. A designer qualified in the House category can prepare the drawings for a secondary suite in a house and submit the permit application."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"When is an architect required?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For larger and more complex buildings, and for certain uses, as set out in Ontario's Architects Act. Houses and some smaller buildings are exempt."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"How can I check a designer's BCIN?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The Province keeps a public registry of qualified designers and firms where you can look up their name, BCIN and qualification categories."}}]}
